CONCLUSION (written by hand after the run above)
- The slope CODE is verified: identical to numpy.gradient on both real DEM windows (max difference 0.0000 deg).
- Against an independent ALGORITHM (Earth Engine's ee.Terrain.slope on the native 30 m DEM), the 10-deg mask puts
  97.8 % (Karambar) and 98.9 % (Atro Sar) of pixels on the same side. The disagreement is not random noise for the
  lake: it sits along the shore, next to steep ground.
- The measured AREA is sensitive to that: Karambar summer passes change by -0.05 % to -0.4 %; Atro Sar summer passes
  by -2.9 % to -3.7 %, and one Atro Sar summer pass (2026-06-12) is refused with Earth Engine's slope; winter and
  spring passes change by 3 % to 14 % (and one spring value, already wrong, collapses further).
- So: the implementation is right, but the radar area depends on how slope is computed near the shoreline, more at
  the smaller Atro Sar. This is one plausible contributor to the unexplained lake-specific radar offsets
  (Karambar +5.2 %, Atro Sar -6.4 %); untested as an explanation.
- Not checkable here: whether 10 deg is the right limit. That needs ground truth (a surveyed or high-resolution
  dated shoreline), which this project does not have.
